te is  a superposition of many positions in  fact all of them equally I'll show how  we motivate this  just like with North assumes we do some  thought experiments about how the state  could be different suppose we have some  superposition of locations we just  imagine what if these places had been a  bit to the right instead we'll call this  offset amount Delta and as you can see  position wave functions are the same but  just Delta to the right remember the way  we define this shift the position is  changed but the speeds the particles are  going at is not so imagine our original  wave function had instead been a  momentum eigenstate then what happens to  that when it shifted we'd expect this  wave function is exactly the same right  because the shift doesn't affect the  speed and so it should still be the same  momentum eigenstate well actually that  doesn't mean the wave function has to be  exactly the same we definitely want it  to be going at the same speed if we  measure it but that still allows a bit  of freedom because remember the  probability of a particular speed is the  length of the coefficient of the complex  number sitting in front of here now  imagine if the state had had a negative  1 in front of it instead negative 1  still has length 1 and so there's still  a probability of 1 what about if there  had been an eye there instead that still  has length 1 as well and so it would be  the same thing in fact if we go back to  thinking of complex numbers as arrows  all complex numbers on this circle have  length 1 in physics we call these  numbers phases there's a really  convenient way to represent a phase  suppose the space has angle theta using  our trigonometry we could write that as  a complex number like this now we're  just going to define the complex  exponential to be this expression  because it's convenient and it means  that we can write down this phase just  using this one more useful fact about  phases that we're going to use a bit  later is 